How to use

---

(object) $onPostSave = new Action(name: 'post_save');

$onPostSave->add(subscriber: new PostSaveSubscriber);

$onPostSave->register();


---

Works for filters too.

The subscriber needs to implement the CouponURLs\Original\Events\Subscriber interface.

The Subscriber is a Duck interface, the implementtation cannot be enforced at compile time because the methods have variable arguments. Nevertheless, all methods described in the interface MUST be implemented; they will be used at runtime.

Eg:

Class PostSaveSubscriber implements Subscriber
{
    public function priority() : int
    {
        return 10;    
    }

    public function createEventArguments(/*here are the original hook arguments*/) : EventArguments
    {

    }

    public function validator(/*The arguments set in createEventArguments() will be passed here, by NAME*/) : Validator
    {

    }

    public function execute(/*The arguments set in createEventArguments() will be passed here, by NAME*/) : void
    {

    }
}

---

Requests

This framework also provides classes to specify that a specific hook be used. These are the Request\* classes. This is useful when you want your client code to specify an unnkown action or filter to use at runtime.

You may use them as:

(object) $savePost = new CouponURLs\Original\Events\Wordpress\Request\Action(name: 'save_post');

An interface somewhere in your code may use this request to create and add a scribscriber for that specific action.

For example:

public function createAction(Request\Action $actionRequest) : Action
{
    $actionRequest->name() // 'save_post'
    $actionRequest->type() // 'CouponURLs\Original\Events\Wordpress\Action'
}

$actionFactory->createAction(new Request\Action(name: 'save_post'));

With these types you can enforce an interface and be sure you'll be working with atcual Hooks.